# Status updates on long work

On any task that runs longer than about five minutes of wall-clock work, post a status line at least every five minutes. A longer silence is a defect: the user cannot tell the difference between hard thinking and a dead session.

A status update is a message to the user. Tool calls scrolling past do not count.

## Format

One or two lines, no headers, no preamble:

`[HH:MM] <what just finished> → <what is running now>`

Examples:

- `[14:22] Backlog pull done (541 rows, 119 in scope) → reading the 29 scenarios.`
- `[14:26] Draft matrix written → running the validator.`
- `[14:31] Review subagent launched → will report when it returns.`

## What each line must contain

1. A concrete fact from the work just completed — a count, a filename, a decision. Never "still working on it" or "making progress."
2. What is running right now.

If a step is genuinely long, say so and say what the completion signal will be.

## Keep the turn open

You can only speak while the turn is open. Ending the turn to wait for a background job or a subagent guarantees a silence of unknown length — the exact failure this rule exists to prevent.

While background work is running and the user is waiting on it, alternate: post a status line, block for about 280 seconds, post the next line. Repeat until the work lands. This overrides any general guidance to end the turn and rely on completion notifications.

Cheap ways to produce a real fact while waiting: list the output directory for sizes and timestamps, count lines in a growing file, or read the tail of a log.

## Do not

- Batch several minutes of work into one recap at the end.
- Pad a status line with reasoning, options, or offers of help.
- Put a question in a status line. Questions get their own message.
- Treat delegation as an excuse for silence. If a subagent is running, report elapsed time and what has come back.
